{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Small intestine cancer is a rare disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the small intestine.","source_span":"Small intestine cancer is a rare disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the small intestine."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"There are five types of small intestine cancer: adenocarcinoma, sarcoma, carcinoid tumors, gastrointestinal stromal tumor, and lymphoma.","source_span":"The types of cancer found in the small intestine are adenocarcinoma, sarcoma, carcinoid tumors, gastrointestinal stromal tumor, and lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Adenocarcinoma starts in glandular cells in the lining of the small intestine and is the most common type of small intestine cancer.","source_span":"Adenocarcinoma starts in glandular cells in the lining of the small intestine and is the most common type of small intestine cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Most adenocarcinoma tumors of the small intestine occur in the part near the stomach.","source_span":"Most of these tumors occur in the part of the small intestine near the stomach."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Leiomyosarcoma starts in the smooth muscle cells of the small intestine.","source_span":"Leiomyosarcoma starts in the smooth muscle cells of the small intestine."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Most leiomyosarcoma tumors occur in the part of the small intestine near the large intestine.","source_span":"Most of these tumors occur in the part of the small intestine near the large intestine."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Risk factors for small intestine cancer include eating a high-fat diet, having Crohn disease, having celiac disease, and having familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP).","source_span":"Risk factors for small intestine cancer include the following: - Eating a high-fat diet. - Having Crohn disease. - Having celiac disease. - Having familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of small intestine cancer include pain or cramps in the middle of the abdomen, unexplained weight loss, a lump in the abdomen, and blood in the stool.","source_span":"Pain or cramps in the middle of the abdomen. - Weight loss with no known reason. - A lump in the abdomen. - Blood in the stool."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Capsule endoscopy involves swallowing a pill-sized capsule with a light and tiny wireless camera that sends pictures to a recorder worn around the waist or shoulder, and the capsule passes out of the body during a bowel movement.","source_span":"A capsule that is about the size of a large pill and contains a light and a tiny wireless camera is swallowed by the patient...sends many pictures of the inside of the digestive tract to a recorder that is worn around the waist or over the shoulder...The capsule passes out of the body during a bowel movement."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","statement":"Double balloon endoscopy uses a special instrument made of two tubes, one inside the other, inserted through the mouth or rectum into the small intestine.","source_span":"A special instrument made up of two tubes (one inside the other) is inserted through the mouth or rectum and into the small intestine."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the five types of small intestine cancer?","answer":"Adenocarcinoma, sarcoma, carcinoid tumors, gastrointestinal stromal tumor, and lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which type of small intestine cancer is most common, and where does it originate?","answer":"Adenocarcinoma is the most common type; it starts in glandular cells in the lining of the small intestine."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the risk factors for developing small intestine cancer?","answer":"Eating a high-fat diet, having Crohn disease, having celiac disease, and having familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What symptoms should prompt someone to see a doctor about possible small intestine cancer?","answer":"Pain or cramps in the middle of the abdomen, unexplained weight loss, a lump in the abdomen, or blood in the stool."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How does capsule endoscopy examine the small intestine?","answer":"The patient swallows a pill-sized capsule containing a light and tiny wireless camera; it sends pictures to a recorder worn on the waist or shoulder and passes out of the body during a bowel movement."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000039_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Where in the small intestine do leiomyosarcoma tumors most commonly occur?","answer":"Most leiomyosarcoma tumors occur in the part of the small intestine near the large intestine."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"Thymoma and thymic carcinoma are diseases in which malignant cells form on the outside surface of the thymus.","source_span":"Thymoma and thymic carcinoma are diseases in which malignant (cancer) cells form on the outside surface of the thymus."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"The thymus is a small organ in the upper chest under the breastbone, is part of the lymph system, and makes lymphocytes that protect the body against infections.","source_span":"The thymus, a small organ that lies in the upper chest under the breastbone, is part of the lymph system. It makes white blood cells, called lymphocytes, that protect the body against infections."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"Thymoma tumor cells grow slowly and rarely spread beyond the thymus, whereas thymic carcinoma cells grow more quickly and have usually spread to other parts of the body by the time the cancer is found.","source_span":"The tumor cells in a thymoma look similar to the normal cells of the thymus, grow slowly, and rarely spread beyond the thymus...the tumor cells in a thymic carcinoma...grow more quickly, and have usually spread to other parts of the body when the cancer is found."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"Autoimmune diseases linked with thymoma include myasthenia gravis, acquired pure red cell aplasia, hypogammaglobulinemia, polymyositis, lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, thyroiditis, and Sjögren syndrome.","source_span":"They include: - Myasthenia gravis. - Acquired pure red cell aplasia. - Hypogammaglobulinemia. - Polymyositis. - Lupus erythematosus. - Rheumatoid arthritis. - Thyroiditis. - Sjgren syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of thymoma and thymic carcinoma include a cough that doesn't go away, chest pain, and trouble breathing.","source_span":"Check with your doctor if you have any of the following: - A cough that doesn't go away. - Chest pain. - Trouble breathing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"A fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy uses a thin needle to remove a sample of cells, while a core biopsy uses a wide needle.","source_span":"using a thin needle to remove a sample of cells. This is called a fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy. Sometimes a wide needle is used to remove a sample of cells and this is called a core biopsy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for thymoma and thymic carcinoma depend on the stage of the cancer, the type of cancer cell, whether the tumor can be removed completely by surgery, the patient's general health, and whether the cancer is newly diagnosed or recurred.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - The stage of the cancer. - The type of cancer cell. - Whether the tumor can be removed completely by surgery. - The patient's general health. - Whether the cancer has just been diagnosed or has recurred (come back)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"Thymoma is staged using four stages: Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV.","source_span":"The following stages are used for thymoma: - Stage I - Stage II - Stage III - Stage IV"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","statement":"Cancer can spread through tissue, the lymph system, and the blood.","source_span":"Cancer can spread through tissue, the lymph system, and the blood"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is the thymus and what is its function?","answer":"The thymus is a small organ in the upper chest under the breastbone, part of the lymph system, that makes lymphocytes (white blood cells) which protect the body against infections."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How do thymoma and thymic carcinoma differ in how they behave?","answer":"Thymoma cells grow slowly and rarely spread beyond the thymus, while thymic carcinoma cells grow more quickly and have usually already spread to other parts of the body by the time they are found."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What autoimmune diseases are commonly linked with thymoma?","answer":"Myasthenia gravis, acquired pure red cell aplasia, hypogammaglobulinemia, polymyositis, lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, thyroiditis, and Sjögren syndrome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What symptoms of thymoma or thymic carcinoma should prompt a visit to the doctor?","answer":"A cough that doesn't go away, chest pain, and trouble breathing."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is the difference between a fine-needle aspiration biopsy and a core biopsy for diagnosing thymoma?","answer":"A fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy uses a thin needle to remove cell samples, while a core biopsy uses a wide needle."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000040_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What five factors affect prognosis and treatment options for thymoma and thymic carcinoma?","answer":"The stage of the cancer, the type of cancer cell, whether the tumor can be removed completely by surgery, the patient's general health, and whether the cancer is newly diagnosed or has recurred."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"Urethral cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the urethra.","source_span":"Urethral cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the urethra."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"In women the urethra is about 1½ inches long and lies just above the vagina; in men it is about 8 inches long and goes through the prostate gland and the penis.","source_span":"In women, the urethra is about 1 inches long and is just above the vagina. In men, the urethra is about 8 inches long, and goes through the prostate gland and the penis to the outside of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"Urethral cancer is a rare cancer that occurs more often in men than in women.","source_span":"Urethral cancer is a rare cancer that occurs more often in men than in women."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of urethral cancer.","source_span":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of urethral cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"Transitional cell carcinoma of the urethra forms in the area near the urethral opening in women, and in the part of the urethra that goes through the prostate gland in men.","source_span":"Transitional cell carcinoma forms in the area near the urethral opening in women, and in the part of the urethra that goes through the prostate gland in men."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"Risk factors for urethral cancer include a history of bladder cancer and conditions causing chronic urethral inflammation, including sexually transmitted diseases such as HPV (especially HPV type 16) and frequent urinary tract infections.","source_span":"Risk factors for urethral cancer include the following: - Having a history of bladder cancer. - Having conditions that cause chronic inflammation in the urethra, including: - Sexually transmitted diseases (STDs), including human papillomavirus (HPV), especially HPV type 16. - Frequent urinary tract infections (UTIs)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"Signs of urethral cancer include bleeding or trouble with urination, and there may be no signs or symptoms in the early stages.","source_span":"Signs of urethral cancer include bleeding or trouble with urination...There may be no signs or symptoms in the early stages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"Urethral cancer can metastasize quickly to tissues around the urethra and is often found in nearby lymph nodes by the time it is diagnosed.","source_span":"Urethral cancer can metastasize (spread) quickly to tissues around the urethra and is often found in nearby lymph nodes by the time it is diagnosed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","statement":"A ureteroscope is a thin, tube-like instrument with a light and lens that is inserted through the urethra into the bladder, ureter, and renal pelvis to check for abnormal areas.","source_span":"A ureteroscope is a thin, tube-like instrument with a light and a lens for viewing. The ureteroscope is inserted through the urethra into the bladder, ureter, and renal pelvis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How does the length of the urethra differ between women and men?","answer":"In women the urethra is about 1½ inches long and lies just above the vagina; in men it is about 8 inches long and passes through the prostate gland and penis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the most common type of urethral cancer?","answer":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of urethral cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the risk factors for urethral cancer?","answer":"A history of bladder cancer, and chronic urethral inflammation from conditions like STDs (especially HPV type 16) and frequent urinary tract infections."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the signs of urethral cancer?","answer":"Bleeding or trouble with urination; there may be no signs or symptoms in the early stages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Why is urethral cancer often advanced by the time it is diagnosed?","answer":"It can metastasize quickly to tissues around the urethra and is often already found in nearby lymph nodes by the time it is diagnosed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000041_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Where does transitional cell carcinoma of the urethra form in men compared to women?","answer":"In women it forms near the urethral opening; in men it forms in the part of the urethra that goes through the prostate gland."}
